  "title": "Arvutimuuseum: Computer Museum in Estonia",
  "date": "2025-09-13T21:00:00.000Z",
  "content": "Arvutimuuseum: Computer Museum in Estonia\n\nToday I dropped by the Arvutimuuseum, the Computer Museum in Estonia. Such a nostalgic trip through the history of computing — from Soviet-era machines to quirky devices that once shaped (and sometimes failed) the future of tech. 🚀\n\n\n📖 A Soviet-era computer book.\n\n\n🍏 A whole wall of old Macs. Apple always had a style.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n🐭 Apple mice — beautiful, but not always practical. Seems like \"form over function\" isn’t new for them.\n\n\n💡 ZX Spectrum — proof that Raspberry Pi’s keyboard PC wasn’t the first “all-in-one” idea.\n\n\n🧵 Magnetic core memory.\n\n\n📇 Punch cards — programming with patience.\n\n\n\n⌨️ Keyboards weren’t always QWERTY.\n\n\n💻 Eee PC — one of my old favorites (alongside VAIO P). Both were defeated by the iPad revolution.\n\n\n\n📱 Speaking of iPad, here’s the Apple Newton.\n\n\n📟 PDAs everywhere — Palm, Psion, and more.\n\n\n⌨️ A Palm foldable keyboard.\n\n\n💿 Ubuntu once mailed you CDs for free. I still have mine.\n\n\n🖊️ A mouse designed like a stylus — not the first attempt either.\n\n\n📀 Storage evolution — floppy, cassette, CD-ROM.\n\n\n💾 The original Save button.\n\n\n\n🖥️ Old servers.\n\n\n🇪🇪 Estonian-made computers!\n\n\n🖨️ Printers & monitors powered by serial ports.\n\n\n🧮 Calculators.\n\n\n🎨 Early graphic computers.\n\n\n🪟 Windows Chicago — no taskbar at first.\n\n\n\n💼 The earliest laptops, one even had a built-in printer.\n\n\n\n🚀 Space laptops — chips specially adjusted for space environments.\n\n\n🇰🇷 Surprised to see Samsung personal computers that early — and wow, the screens were already impressive.\n\n\nSuch a fun dive into tech history. Every piece felt like a step that led us to the devices we casually use today. 🖥️📱💿",
